# StrictDoc's export and Python's cache. Both are regenerated on every run and
# neither is source.
#
# Only output/strictdoc/ is excluded, not output/ itself: the folder may well
# hold something that was put there on purpose. The leading **/ makes the rule
# reach every spec folder under maintenance/, which one anchored rule cannot do
# from here.
#
# Anything that writes into a spec folder must aim at output/strictdoc/. A bare
# "strictdoc export <folder>" with no --output-dir scatters _cache/ and html/
# straight into output/, which this does not catch - and that showing up in
# git status is the signal that the wrong command was used.

**/output/strictdoc/
__pycache__/

# The two below are NOT generated output, and they are a pair - track both or
# ignore both, never one of them.
#
#   strictdoc_config.py   Configuration. It carries project_title and the set of
#                         screens that are enabled, and it is meant to be edited.
#   strictdoc-theme.css   Colour, rewritten by StrictDocStarter on every open.
#                         strictdoc_config.py names it in custom_css_path, and
#                         StrictDoc validates that path: with the config present
#                         and the theme missing, export fails outright with
#                         "config: custom_css_path: invalid path to a CSS file".
#
# They are ignored here because the folders under maintenance/ are measurement
# snapshots - what they have to prove is that the export passes and the chain is
# right, which needs neither file. Dropping the config costs three of StrictDoc's
# screens (measured: 8 HTML files become 5); that is acceptable for a snapshot
# and would not be for a real project.
#
# A project that people actually open must track both. See
# prompt/handoff-2026-08-09-strictdoc-starter.md section 4.

strictdoc_config.py
strictdoc-theme.css
